  <LessonChrome slug="the-birth-of-civilization">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>The Birth of Civilization</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Today we lay the ground beneath the foundation. The man this course
        builds has not appeared yet. The rings here are household, clan, town,
        and palace-state.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="learn" aria-labelledby="will-learn">
      <h2 id="will-learn">What you’ll learn</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>How people moved from hunting and gathering to farming, and why that change made everything else possible.</li>
        <li>How farmers reached Crete, and how their households grew into clans, towns, and finally palace-states.</li>
        <li>What the Minoan palaces were, what they achieved, and what we still do not know about them.</li>
        <li>How Minoan civilization was damaged, taken over, and finally lost, and what that teaches.</li>
        <li>Why this story is the ground beneath the foundation of Greek civilization, and of Homer.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="lesson-body">
      <p class="scene-today">Today we lay the ground beneath the foundation.</p>
      <p>
        Before we build the man, we must understand the ground he stands on. In
        Module I.1 we will meet Achilles, the warrior who cared above all for
        honor. But Achilles did not appear out of nowhere. Behind him lay
        thousands of years in which human beings learned to farm, to settle, to
        build, to trade, to write, and to govern. Behind him, too, lay the memory
        of a great island civilization that rose and fell before the Greeks of
        Homer’s day were born.
      </p>
      <p>
        That civilization is Minoan Crete. It is the first great civilization of
        Europe, and its story is short enough to tell in one lesson and rich
        enough to teach us something about every later age. It also follows, very
        closely, the pattern of this course. In Sophandros we follow a man as his
        circle of responsibility widens: from himself, to his household, to his
        city, to his country, and beyond. In this lesson we watch whole societies
        take those first steps for the first time: from the single household, to
        the clan, to the town, to the palace-state.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read this lesson straight through, as you would a chapter in a textbook.
        I will explain each stage, tell you what the evidence is, and tell you
        plainly where scholars disagree.
      </p>

      <h3>1. Before farming: the hunter-gatherers</h3>
      <p>
        For almost all of human history, people lived by hunting animals and
        gathering wild plants. They moved with the seasons, following herds and
        ripening foods. Groups were small, usually a few families, perhaps a few
        dozen people. Everyone knew everyone. There were no kings, no cities, and
        no writing.
      </p>
      <p>
        This does not mean these people were simple. They made fine stone tools,
        sewed clothing, controlled fire, buried their dead with care, and painted
        the walls of caves. They knew their landscapes in remarkable detail. But
        their way of life set limits. You cannot store much food if you are always
        moving. You cannot build a large house if you will leave it next season.
        And you cannot support many people who do not themselves find food, such
        as full-time priests, craftsmen, or rulers.
      </p>
      <p>
        The circle of responsibility was narrow: the self and the small band. That
        would change with one of the greatest turning points in human history.
      </p>

      <h3>2. The Neolithic revolution: farming begins</h3>
      <p>
        About 11,500 years ago, after the end of the last Ice Age, people in a
        region of the Near East called the Fertile Crescent began to farm. The
        Fertile Crescent is the arc of land that runs from the Levant (modern
        Israel, Palestine, Lebanon, and western Syria) up into southeastern Turkey
        and down through Mesopotamia (modern Iraq). Here grew the wild ancestors
        of wheat, barley, lentils, and peas, and here lived wild sheep, goats,
        cattle, and pigs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Over many centuries, people learned to plant, harvest, and replant these
        crops, and to tame and breed these animals. Archaeologists call this the
        Neolithic revolution, from the Greek words for “new stone” age. The word
        “revolution” is a little misleading: the change happened slowly, over
        thousands of years, and in different places at different times. But its
        effects were revolutionary.
      </p>
      <p>
        Farming produced more food from the same land. More food meant more
        people. Farmers had to stay near their fields, so they built permanent
        houses and villages. They stored grain, so they needed pots, granaries,
        and ways of keeping track of what belonged to whom. Some people could now
        spend their time on crafts, building, or religion instead of finding food.
        The household, a family living in one house and working its own fields and
        animals, became the basic unit of life. In the language of this course,
        the second ring, the household, was born.
      </p>
      <p>Two famous sites in Anatolia (modern Turkey) show how remarkable this period was.</p>

      <h3>Göbekli Tepe: monuments before cities</h3>
      <p>
        On a hilltop in southeastern Turkey stands Göbekli Tepe. From 1995 the
        German archaeologist Klaus Schmidt excavated large round and oval
        enclosures there, ringed by huge T-shaped stone pillars, some over five
        meters tall and carved with foxes, boars, snakes, vultures, and scorpions.
        Some pillars have arms and hands carved on their sides, as if they
        represent human or godlike figures.
      </p>
      <p>
        The oldest enclosures date to roughly 9600–8200 BC. Those dates come from
        radiocarbon, and ongoing excavation keeps refining them, so treat the span
        as debated. That is thousands of years before writing, metal tools, or the
        first cities. Schmidt interpreted Göbekli Tepe as a sanctuary, a place
        where hunter-gatherers gathered for rituals and feasts, and he argued that
        the effort of building it may have encouraged people to settle and farm.
        Later excavators have found evidence of houses and daily activity there as
        well, so the “temple without a village” picture is now debated. Either
        way, Göbekli Tepe shows that people could organize large, shared projects
        at the very start of the farming age.
      </p>

      <h3>Çatalhöyük: a town of households</h3>
      <p>
        Further west, on the Konya plain of central Turkey, lies Çatalhöyük, one
        of the largest early farming settlements ever found. It was occupied from
        roughly 7100 to 6000 BC; that span is debated as excavation continues. It
        was first excavated by James Mellaart in the 1960s and then, from 1993, by
        a long project led by Ian Hodder.
      </p>
      <p>
        Çatalhöyük had no streets. Its mud-brick houses were packed so tightly
        together that people walked across the rooftops and climbed down into
        their homes by ladders. Houses were rebuilt again and again on the same
        spot. Inside, people plastered and painted the walls, set cattle horns
        into benches and walls, and buried their dead beneath the floors. Several
        thousand people may have lived there at once. Estimates vary widely, so
        the number is debated.
      </p>
      <p>
        What Hodder’s team noticed is just as important as what they found: there
        was no sign of palaces, temples standing apart from houses, or a ruling
        class. Houses were much alike. Çatalhöyük was a town made of households,
        without a central government. It shows that people could live together in
        large numbers long before anyone ruled over them. That central power would
        come later, and we will see it arise on Crete.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Farmers cross the sea to Crete</h3>
      <p>
        Farming spread out of Anatolia and the Near East over the following
        millennia. Some of the people who carried it went by sea.
      </p>
      <p>
        Crete is a large, mountainous island in the southern Aegean Sea, about 260
        kilometers long, lying between Greece, Turkey, and North Africa. Its first
        lasting settlers arrived around 7000 BC. The date rests on radiocarbon
        from the lowest levels at Knossos and continues to be refined, so it is
        debated. They settled at Knossos, in the north-central part of the island,
        on the spot where a palace would stand some five thousand years later.
      </p>
      <p>
        These settlers did not slowly become farmers on Crete. They arrived as
        farmers. They brought with them wheat and barley, and sheep, goats, pigs,
        and cattle, animals that were not native to the island. That means they
        came by boat, deliberately, carrying seed grain and live animals across
        open water. It was a planned migration by households who meant to stay.
      </p>
      <p>
        Modern genetics supports this picture. In 2017 a team led by Iosif
        Lazaridis published a study of ancient DNA in the journal <em>Nature</em>
        (“Genetic origins of the Minoans and Mycenaeans”). They found that both
        Minoans and the later Mycenaean Greeks descended mostly from early
        Neolithic farmers related to those of western Anatolia. The Minoans also
        carried some ancestry related to peoples further east, from the Caucasus
        and Iran. The Mycenaeans carried that too, plus an extra share related to
        peoples of the Eurasian steppe. In plain words: the people of Bronze Age
        Crete were, in large part, the descendants of those first farming
        households who crossed the sea.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the next three thousand years, Neolithic Crete was a world of
        villages. The settlement at Knossos grew slowly, layer upon layer, into
        one of the largest Neolithic sites in the Aegean. Life centered on the
        household, its fields and its flocks.
      </p>

      <h3>4. Households, villages, and clans: Early Minoan Crete</h3>
      <p>
        Around 3100 BC, the people of Crete began to use copper and then bronze,
        an alloy of copper with tin or arsenic. Dates for the start of the Bronze
        Age on Crete vary by a century or so, so that opening date is debated.
        Archaeologists call the period from then until about 2000 BC the Early
        Minoan period. The name “Minoan” is modern. It was coined by the British
        archaeologist Sir Arthur Evans, who excavated Knossos from 1900, after
        Minos, the legendary king of Crete in Greek myth. We do not know what the
        people called themselves.
      </p>
      <p>
        In this period, the household was joined by a wider ring: the clan, a
        group of related families who shared ancestors, land, and obligations.
      </p>

      <h3>The tombs of the Mesara</h3>
      <p>
        The clearest evidence comes from the Mesara plain in southern Crete. Here
        communities built tholos tombs: round stone tombs with a single doorway,
        used for burial over many generations, sometimes for centuries. Hundreds
        of people might be laid to rest in one tomb, along with pottery, stone
        vessels, seals, and ornaments. Outside, there were often paved areas where
        the living seem to have gathered for rites.
      </p>
      <p>
        A tomb used by one group for centuries tells us something about how people
        saw themselves. They were not only individual families but members of a
        lineage that stretched back to the ancestors and forward to the unborn.
        Shared tombs bound families together and gave them a claim to their land.
        The circle of responsibility had widened from the household to the clan.
      </p>

      <h3>Myrtos: a hilltop settlement</h3>
      <p>
        At Myrtos (Fournou Korifi) on the south coast, the archaeologist Peter
        Warren excavated a small Early Minoan settlement in the 1960s: a cluster
        of around ninety rooms on a hilltop, destroyed by fire around 2200 BC.
        That destruction date is debated. Warren first interpreted it as a single
        communal household of perhaps a hundred people, with shared storage,
        cooking, and weaving. Later scholars, especially Todd Whitelaw,
        reinterpreted it as a hamlet of several separate households living side by
        side. That reading is debated. The debate itself is instructive: it is
        the question of when separate households combine into a larger community,
        and how archaeologists tell the difference.
      </p>

      <h3>Bronze, ships, and the wider world</h3>
      <p>
        Bronze changed the economy. Tin does not occur on Crete, so it had to be
        imported, probably from far away. Sources in the east are most often
        suggested, and that suggestion is debated. Copper came from places such as
        Cyprus and the Cyclades; that, too, is the usual account and is debated.
        To get metal, Cretans had to trade, and
        to trade they had to sail.
      </p>
      <p>
        Crete sits in the middle of the eastern Mediterranean, and its people made
        the most of that. They traded with the Cyclades, the ring of small islands
        to the north, famous for their marble figurines; with the coasts of
        Anatolia and the Levant; and, eventually, with Egypt. Egyptian stone
        vessels and scarabs appear on Crete, and Cretan goods appear in Egypt.
        Later Egyptian texts and tomb paintings refer to people from a land called
        Keftiu, generally identified with Crete. That identification is the usual
        one, and it is still debated.
      </p>
      <p>
        Trade brought wealth, new ideas, and new needs. It also rewarded those who
        could organize it: collect goods, build ships, keep accounts, and deal
        with foreigners. That is how the next ring came into being.
      </p>

      <h3>5. The first palaces: the palace-state (about 1900 BC)</h3>
      <p>
        Around 1900 BC, a date that is debated, in the period archaeologists call
        Middle Minoan, the Cretans built something new: large, complex buildings
        that Evans and later scholars called palaces. The first were at Knossos,
        Phaistos in the south, and Malia on the north coast; others followed.
      </p>
      <p>
        A Minoan palace was not just a ruler’s home. Each was built around a large
        rectangular central court, with wings of rooms on every side: storerooms
        filled with huge clay jars, pithoi, for grain, oil, and wine; workshops;
        shrines; halls for gatherings; and grand staircases and upper floors. Most
        were oriented on a similar plan, which suggests shared ideas about how
        such a building should work.
      </p>
      <p>
        Their main economic role, most scholars agree, was redistribution. Produce
        from farms over a wide area flowed into the palace stores: grain, oil,
        wine, wool. The palace then supported craftsmen, officials, and workers,
        fed rituals and feasts, and traded surplus goods abroad. This made the
        palace the center of a territory, not just of a town. We can fairly call
        these early palace-states: the fourth ring, after household, clan, and
        town.
      </p>
      <p>
        Such a system needs records. The Minoans developed two writing systems:
        Cretan hieroglyphic, used mainly on seals, and Linear A, written on clay
        tablets and other objects. Linear A seems to have been used largely for
        accounts: lists of goods and quantities. We can read some of its numbers
        and guess at some signs, because the later Linear B script borrowed from
        it, but Linear A has not been deciphered, and we do not know what language
        it records. When you read confident claims about what Minoans believed or
        how they were governed, remember: we have never read a Minoan explaining
        it in his own words.
      </p>

      <h3>6. Destruction and rebuilding: the Neopalatial age (about 1700–1450 BC)</h3>
      <p>
        Around 1700 BC, a date that is debated, the first palaces were destroyed.
        Many scholars attribute this to a major earthquake, since Crete lies in an
        active earthquake zone and the destruction was widespread. Some have
        suggested conflict as a cause or a contributing factor, so the cause is
        debated. What is remarkable is what came next. The Minoans rebuilt, and
        rebuilt grander. Archaeologists call this the Neopalatial (“new palace”)
        period, and it is the height of Minoan civilization.
      </p>

      <h3>Art and daily life</h3>
      <p>
        The new palaces and the great houses (“villas”) around them were decorated
        with frescoes, paintings on wet plaster, showing processions, women in
        elaborate dress, lilies, monkeys, dolphins, and the sea. Minoan art is
        known for its sense of movement and its love of nature. A caution is
        needed: many famous Knossos frescoes survive only as fragments and were
        heavily restored, even partly reimagined, by artists working for Evans in
        the early twentieth century. What you see in the museum is often part
        ancient and part modern reconstruction.
      </p>
      <p>
        The palaces also show impressive engineering. At Knossos there were
        stone-lined drains, terracotta pipes, and light-wells to bring air and
        daylight into inner rooms. Evans called one suite the “Queen’s Megaron”
        and identified a room with a drain as a toilet. The identification is
        plausible but debated in detail. It is fair to say that Minoan builders
        managed water and light with real sophistication.
      </p>

      <h3>Bull-leaping and religion</h3>
      <p>
        Bulls are everywhere in Minoan art. The best-known image, the Bull-Leaping
        Fresco from Knossos, shows figures grasping a charging bull’s horns and
        vaulting over its back. Whether this was a sport, a ritual, or both, and
        whether it was ever done exactly as shown, is debated. Bull horns in stone
        (Evans called them “horns of consecration”) crowned buildings and altars,
        and the double axe, the labrys, appears as a sacred symbol.
      </p>
      <p>
        Minoan religion was practiced in palace shrines, in caves, and at peak
        sanctuaries on mountaintops, where people left clay figurines of people
        and animals. Female figures are prominent in Minoan art, including the
        famous faience “snake goddess” figurines from Knossos. These too were
        partly restored, and some “Minoan” ivory figurines in museum collections
        are now considered modern forgeries. Many scholars think a goddess or goddesses held a central
        place in Minoan religion, but without readable texts we cannot know their
        names or beliefs.
      </p>

      <h3>The island world of Thera</h3>
      <p>
        North of Crete lies the volcanic island of Thera (modern Santorini). There,
        at Akrotiri, the Greek archaeologist Spyridon Marinatos began excavating
        in 1967 a Bronze Age town buried in volcanic ash, preserved like Pompeii.
        Its houses, some three stories high, held wonderful frescoes of ships,
        fishermen, boxers, and landscapes, deeply influenced by Minoan culture.
        Akrotiri shows that the Minoan world reached across the southern Aegean.
      </p>

      <h3>Who ruled?</h3>
      <p>Here we reach one of the great open questions. We do not know who ruled Minoan Crete.</p>
      <p>
        Evans imagined a priest-king, a “Minos,” ruling from Knossos, and named
        rooms accordingly (“Throne Room”). Others have proposed rule by a priestly
        class, by a council of leading families, by several independent
        palace-states rather than one, or by a queen or priestesses, given how
        often women appear in ritual scenes. All of these are proposals, and the
        question is debated. The art shows remarkably few images of a single ruler
        or of war, compared with Egypt or the Near East. Some scholars have taken
        this to mean Minoan society was peaceful; others point out that defensive
        sites and weapons do exist, and that absence of evidence is not evidence
        of absence. The honest answer is that the palace-state is clear, but its
        government is not.
      </p>

      <h3>7. The eruption of Thera</h3>
      <p>
        At some point during the Neopalatial period, the volcano on Thera erupted
        in one of the largest eruptions of the last several thousand years. It
        buried Akrotiri. Its people seem to have left in time; few bodies have
        been found. It threw ash across the eastern Mediterranean, and may have
        caused tsunamis that struck the north coast of Crete. That “may” is debated.
      </p>
      <p>
        The date is one of the great debates of Aegean archaeology. Radiocarbon
        dating, including dates from an olive branch buried by the eruption, has
        pointed to the later seventeenth century BC, roughly 1630–1600 BC.
        Traditional dating based on links with Egyptian history placed it later,
        around 1500 BC. More recent radiocarbon work has suggested a date in the
        late seventeenth or the sixteenth century BC. The matter is not settled,
        and you should treat any single date with caution.
      </p>
      <p>
        The eruption once seemed to explain the end of Minoan civilization: in
        1939 Marinatos proposed that it had destroyed Crete. But the Minoan
        palaces carried on for a long time after the eruption, perhaps for several
        generations. That interval is debated. Most scholars now think the
        eruption weakened Minoan Crete, through damage to coasts, fleets, harvests,
        or trade, rather than destroying it outright. That reading is the current
        majority view, and it is still a judgment. The final blows came later.
      </p>

      <h3>8. Destruction and takeover (about 1450 BC)</h3>
      <p>
        Around 1450 BC, a date that is debated, many sites across Crete were
        destroyed by fire: palaces such as Phaistos and Malia, and villas and
        towns across the island. Knossos alone continued as a palace. The cause is
        debated: earthquake, internal conflict between Cretan centers, or invasion.
      </p>
      <p>
        What happened afterward is clearer. The culture of Knossos changed.
        Warrior burials with weapons appear around it. Pottery and art shift
        toward styles found on the Greek mainland. And the palace scribes began
        writing in a new script, Linear B.
      </p>
      <p>
        Linear B was adapted from Linear A, but it records an early form of Greek.
        This was proved in 1952, when the English architect and codebreaker
        Michael Ventris deciphered it. The thousands of Linear B tablets found at
        Knossos are inventories: sheep and wool, chariots, armor, land, offerings
        to gods, some of whom (Zeus, Poseidon, Athena, under earlier forms) are
        familiar from later Greek religion. The match to those later names is
        debated. The people now running Knossos were Greek speakers from the
        mainland: the Mycenaeans, named after their great citadel of Mycenae.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the Minoan palace-state did not simply vanish. It was taken over by a
        more warlike people, who kept its administrative system, adapted its
        writing for their own language, and ran it for their own purposes.
      </p>

      <h3>9. The final fall</h3>
      <p>
        The Mycenaean palace at Knossos itself was destroyed later, probably in
        the fourteenth century BC. The date of this destruction and of the Knossos
        tablets is one of the most argued questions in the field. Some scholars
        place it around 1375–1350 BC, others later. Treat the date as debated.
        Life on Crete went on, but the great palace system was over.
      </p>
      <p>
        By around 1200 BC, the whole eastern Mediterranean was in crisis. The
        palace kingdoms of mainland Greece, the Hittite empire in Anatolia, and
        many cities of the Levant were destroyed or abandoned within a few
        decades. Egypt survived, but weakened. The historian and archaeologist
        Eric Cline describes this in <em>1177 B.C.: The Year Civilization
        Collapsed</em>, arguing that no single cause explains it. Instead, a
        combination of earthquakes, drought, famine, migration, raiding, and the
        breakdown of trade networks overwhelmed an interconnected system. On
        Crete, people moved to defensible hilltop settlements, such as Karphi high
        in the Lasithi mountains. Karphi is the example named here; the detail of
        that move is debated.
      </p>
      <p>
        Writing disappeared from Greece and Crete for several centuries. The
        Linear scripts were forgotten. When the Greeks learned to write again, it
        was with a new alphabet borrowed from the Phoenicians, as we will see in
        Module I.2.
      </p>

      <h3>10. From Crete to Mycenae and Homer</h3>
      <p>
        What survived was memory. Later Greeks told stories of King Minos, who
        ruled the seas from Knossos; of the Labyrinth built by the craftsman
        Daedalus; of the Minotaur, half man and half bull, to whom Athenian youths
        were sacrificed until Theseus killed it. Homer’s <em>Iliad</em> names
        Knossos and the Cretan leader Idomeneus among the Greeks who sailed to
        Troy. The <em>Odyssey</em> is said to call Crete a land of ninety cities
        in Book 19. That line is not quoted here: it has not been checked against
        the text, and this lesson will not invent it. These are legends, not
        history. Yet it is striking that the myths remember bulls, a great palace
        that one could get lost in, and a sea power centered on Knossos, all
        things archaeology has found.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Mycenaeans who took over Knossos are the world that Homer’s poems
        remember, dimly and from centuries later. In the next lesson,
        <a href="/lessons/the-world-of-homer">The World of Homer</a>, we turn to
        them: their citadels, their warriors, their collapse, and the poets who
        kept their memory alive. Out of that world comes Achilles, and the first
        layer of the man: honor.
      </p>
    </div>

